e productive model: Weak: pasting five sentences. Strong: Maya whispers, ''Not yet…'' (ch.3), showing delayed action. Ellipsis marks omitted words without twisting meaning.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach would weaken your work, then write a corrected version.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: Correct marks, optional ellipsis if shortened fairly, citation included.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"A superficial change in length or appearance does not repair meaning. Changing quote words to fit your claim. Accuracy is non-negotiable. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task.","options":["A guess with no explanation.","Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity.","A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about quotation marks?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nUsing evidence means weaving short quotations with quotation marks, using ellipsis carefully when shortening and citing chapter or page per class rules. Prefer brief phrases over huge block quotes in paragraphs. Now choose different people, facts or text to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake one fresh example demonstrating the skill and explain your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should meet the same purpose as the model, but contain original material.","explain":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new content. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Transfer to an independent example"}],"title":"Year 7, Week 10, Lesson 3: Using evidence and quotes","unit_number":3,"unit_title":"Novel study","week":10,"week_in_unit":2,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nWeak: pasting five sentences. Strong: Maya whispers, ''Not yet…'' (ch.3), showing delayed action. Ellipsis marks omitted words without twisting meaning.\n\nExplain what makes this example accurate and effective, identifying its specific wording.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Using quotations.","caption":"Weave, do not dump.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\